311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1210 0 R
/E (A 1988 amendment to the Fair Housing Act known as the Thurmond Amendment enables landlords to deny housing to individuals convicted of selling and manufacturing drugs. Efforts are underway at state and federal levels to repeal this amendment, and the lifetime of discrimination it conveys. It is unknown how many people have been denied housing under Thurmond, but national estimates of potentially impacted individuals tops three million. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1211 0 R
/E (This report estimates that 91,644 people were convicted of drug selling and manufacturing charges between 2007 and 2025 in Pennsylvania, and could have potentially been denied housing under the Thurmond Amendment. Given the time period constraints and limitations of criminal-legal databases, this is a significant undercounting. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1212 0 R
/E (Thurmond-eligible convictions occur throughout Pennsylvania, and tend to concentrate in the more populated corners of Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, Erie, and Scranton. Convictions in Philadelphia courts accounted for 34% of statewide totals. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1213 0 R
/E (Due to a variety of factors, including shifting legal and cultural landscapes around illegal drugs, Thurmond-eligible convictions have decreased statewide since 2007, with some year-to-year fluctuation. From 2007-2025, an average of 4,824 people have been convicted each year, though the statewide totals have been well below that since 2019. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1214 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5 843 0 R 7]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [6]
/Pg 12 0 R
/P 843 0 R
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1215 0 R
/E (Stable housing is a significant predictor of successful reentry. Repealing the Thurmond Amendment is important to public safety, as well as racial and economic justice. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
