341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 216 0 R
/T (Letter from DA Larry Krasner:)
/E (Letter from DA Larry Krasner:)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Stable housing is one of the strongest predictors of successful reentry and reduced recidivism. The Thurmond Amendment of 1988, a line in the Fair Housing Act allowing lawful discrimination by landlords against people convicted of drug selling or manufacturing, was authored and championed by a staunch segregationist at the height of the War on Drugs. Despite social progress in the nearly 40 years since, this law remains on the books and continues to impact an untold number of people today. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (This is a law that permanently disqualifies people from housing protections based on certain drug convictions, regardless of the severity of the offense or the person's rehabilitation since. Rather than contribute positively to healthy communities, this law works directly against the goals of public safety that our office is charged with prioritizing. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (This report examines the impact of the Thurmond Amendment on Pennsylvanians convicted of drug-related offenses and finds that nearly 100,000 people in our state have been potentially impacted since 2007 alone. These are not just numbers on a page; these are parents trying to rent an apartment in a safer neighborhood, in a better school district, with enough room for their kids to play. These are essential workers trying to secure housing closer to their jobsite. These are elderly people seeking more accessible housing as they age. A person convicted of a Thurmond-eligible conviction can be denied housing repeatedly throughout their lifetime, and no one is keeping score. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
