387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (In the hierarchy of basic human needs, housing ranks highly. Safe and stable housing is a major predictor of success for individuals with past criminal legal involvement, particularly those reentering society after incarceration.\(1\) Despite the direct ties between housing and public safety, laws targeting past convictions enable discriminatory practices and destabilize communities. Under a 1988 amendment in the Fair Housing Act  known as the Thurmond Amendment landlords can deny housing to anyone with a past drug crime conviction, no matter the type or quantity of drugs involved. This collateral consequence is lifelong and far-reaching, preventing individuals from establishing the building blocks of a successful future, increasing likelihood of recidivism, and deteriorating public safety at its foundation. A relic of the War on Drugs, the Thurmond Amendment has impacted an estimated three million Americans convicted of drug crimes since 1988, and likely many more. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
